<!-- ============== WHY ============== -->
<section class="sec" id="why">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01</span><h2>Why a 27B model at one bit</h2></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Edge inference is a memory problem before it is a compute problem. A model that does not
        fit in DRAM does not run at all, and quantization is the only lever that moves a model by
        an order of magnitude.
      </p>
      <p>
        The interesting consequence is what you can buy at a fixed budget. Around four gigabytes,
        the choice is not "a 27B model or nothing" - it is <em>which 27B</em>, or a much smaller
        model at higher precision:
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Weights only, at one memory budget</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Format</th><th>Bytes per weight</th><th>Weights</th><th>Memory</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Bonsai-4B</td><td>Q8_0</td><td>1.06</td><td>4 G</td><td>4.25 GB</td></tr>
          <tr class="hi"><td>Bonsai-27B</td><td>Q1_0</td><td>0.14</td><td>27 G</td><td>3.80 GB</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <p>
        <strong>6.75x the parameters, in less memory.</strong> That is the bet behind 1-bit
        quantization: that parameter count buys more than per-parameter precision. Whether it
        pays off in output quality is a model question, and one this article does not answer - we
        measured speed, not perplexity.
      </p>
      <p>
        What we can answer is the other half, and it is the half that decides whether the bet is
        even worth making. A 1-bit model is only useful if the arithmetic runs fast enough to be
        worth having, and by default it does not: the stock kernel leaves three quarters of this
        core's vector unit idle. On the node measured below, that is the difference between
        <strong>54 million cycles and 4.6 million</strong> for the same result.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============== RESULT ============== -->
<section class="sec" id="result">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02</span><h2>The core, and what runs on it</h2></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Four things about the AX46MPV decide everything below. Each one is load-bearing for a
        later claim, so they are worth stating before the numbers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="kv caps">
      <li><span class="k">One hart</span><span class="v">Every measurement is single-threaded.
        There is no second core to hide a slow kernel behind.</span></li>
      <li><span class="k">VLEN 1024</span><span class="v">One vector register is 128 int8 lanes,
        or 64 int32 lanes at LMUL=2. That 64 is exactly the number of weight rows the repacked
        layout puts in flight, which is why change 01 works.</span></li>
      <li><span class="k">XAndesVDot, stock</span><span class="v">A 4-way int8 dot product is
        already in this core's ISA string. Change 03 uses it as shipped - no custom silicon.</span></li>
      <li><span class="k">Thin memory</span><span class="v">32 KB L1 data, no L2 at all, 2 MB L3.
        A kernel that streams weights once has nothing to reuse, which is why change 02 is a
        prefetch and not a blocking change.</span></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <p>
        Three Bonsai models quantized to Q1_0 run end to end on a single AX46MPV hart and produce
        coherent text. The largest is <strong>3.8 GB of 1-bit weights across 64 layers</strong>,
        driven by one core with no second thread to hide behind.
      </p>
      <p>
        The table is one MUL_MAT node per model, measured four times as the kernel was rebuilt.
        Decode is the interesting direction: it reads every weight once per token and reuses
        nothing, so it is the case that actually limits an edge device.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Decode cycles, one MUL_MAT node, single hart</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Node</th><th>Upstream</th><th>Optimized</th><th>Speedup</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Bonsai-4B</td><td>blk.7.attn_v</td><td>4,476,673</td><td>362,799</td><td>12.34x</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bonsai-8B</td><td>blk.7.attn_v</td><td>7,278,168</td><td>648,246</td><td>11.23x</td></tr>
          <tr class="hi"><td>Bonsai-27B</td><td>blk.6.attn_gate</td><td>54,314,202</td><td>4,578,346</td><td>11.86x</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <details class="more">
      <summary>What "runs end to end" means here, precisely</summary>
      <div class="dbody">
        <p>
          Two different runs back two different claims, and it is worth keeping them apart.
          Correctness comes from the functional model: the whole graph, every token, all 497
          MUL_MAT nodes of the 27B model, which prints its answer in about 15 minutes of wall
          clock. Cycle counts come from RTL, which measures one kernel at a time.
        </p>
        <p>
          They cannot be the same run. The RTL engine advances roughly 3.8 thousand cycles per
          second, and a single 27B token is about 3.9 billion cycles - twelve days for one token.
          The measurement method in section 05 exists precisely because of that gap.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============== PROBLEM ============== -->
<section class="sec" id="problem">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">03</span><h2>Why a 1-bit matmul is a layout problem</h2></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A Q1_0 block holds <strong>128 weights in 16 bytes</strong>, one bit each, plus a single
        fp16 scale. A set bit means +1, a clear bit means -1. The activations are 8-bit.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the inner operation is not a multiply-accumulate. It is <em>add or subtract the
        activation</em>. There is no arithmetic left to make cheaper, which means the entire cost
        of the kernel is decided by two things: how many lanes carry useful work, and how many
        instructions it takes to feed them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The stock kernel loses on both. A Q1_0 block is one row of 128 columns, so the natural
        loop reads part of one row, multiplies it against the activations, and then has to add
        those products together - numbers sitting in different lanes of the same register. That
        last step is a <strong>horizontal reduction</strong>, and it is serial.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="figbox">
      {{FIG:lanes}}
      <p class="figcap">
        <b>The cost, to scale.</b> One step of the stock kernel fills 32 of the machine's 128
        byte-lanes and advances a single output row, then pays a serial reduction to collapse it.
        Transposing the weights puts one output row in every lane; the Andes dot widens the group
        to 256 bytes and keeps all 64 rows moving. Nothing here is scaled down.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="pull">
      <p>
        Every lane the step leaves idle and every reduction it performs is a consequence of the
        file format's row-major layout. You cannot tune your way out of it. The data has to move.
      </p>
    </div>

    <details class="more">
      <summary>The two block formats, byte by byte</summary>
      <div class="dbody">
        <p>
          <code>block_q1_0</code> is 18 bytes: one fp16 scale then <code>qs[16]</code>, whose bit
          <em>c</em> is column <em>c</em> of a single row. Rows follow one another in memory.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>block_q8_0</code> is 34 bytes: one fp16 scale then 32 int8 quants. Note that
          <code>qs</code> starts at byte offset 2 - this core requires a 32-bit vector element
          load to be 4-byte aligned, so the activation quants are restaged into an aligned buffer
          once per call before the wide dot can read them as words.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============== MOVES ============== -->
<section class="sec" id="moves">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">04</span><h2>The four changes</h2></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Each one removes the bottleneck the previous one exposed. Three are portable RVV and
        apply to any compliant core; one uses a stock Andes V5 vendor instruction.
      </p>
      <p>
        The gain quoted on each card is that change alone, taken from the <strong>4B ladder</strong> -
        the one model where every intermediate build was measured, so the four multiply back to
        its 12.34x end to end.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="k">01 &nbsp;portable RVV</p>
        <h3>Transpose the weights into the lanes</h3>
        <p>
          Repack 64 rows into one <code>block_q1_0x64</code> so that lane <em>j</em> holds row
          <em>j</em>. Summing over K becomes a vertical add inside each lane, and one 8-byte mask
          load supplies a whole column across all 64 rows. Same bytes, permuted at load time.
        </p>
        <p class="won">Every lane busy, zero horizontal reductions &nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp; 2.09x</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="k">02 &nbsp;portable RVV</p>
        <h3>Prefetch the weight stream</h3>
        <p>
          With the arithmetic nearly free, the kernel is waiting on memory: it streams 1152 fresh
          bytes per block and never reads them again. One <code>zicbop</code> hint per cache line,
          one block ahead, covers the latency the hardware prefetcher cannot.
        </p>
        <p class="won">Stalls hidden on the decode path &nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp; 1.54x on top</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="k">03 &nbsp;Andes V5</p>
        <h3>Hand the reduction to nds.vd4dots.vv</h3>
        <p>
          A stock XAndesVDot instruction: each 32-bit lane accumulates four int8 products. At
          VLEN=1024 the <code>e32/m2</code> group is exactly 64 lanes - 64 output rows - and the
          same registers seen as bytes are 64 rows by 4 columns. The operand shape and the
          repacked tile are the same object.
        </p>
        <p class="won">256 multiply-accumulates per instruction &nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp; 3.04x on top</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="k">04 &nbsp;codegen</p>
        <h3>Stop re-describing the registers</h3>
        <p>
          The loop alternates between a byte view for the mask and a 32-bit view for the dot, and
          every alternation costs a <code>vsetvli</code> that does no arithmetic. Keeping all the
          32-bit work inside one asm block leaves the compiler only byte-width work to schedule.
        </p>
        <p class="won">4.00 down to 0.94 vsetvli per dot &nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp; 1.26x on top</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <details class="more">
      <summary>Why the repack is free at run time, and what it costs at load</summary>
      <div class="dbody">
        <p>
          The permutation happens once, in the buffer's <code>set_tensor</code> while the model
          file is being read, so no graph execution ever pays for it. The repacked block is
          exactly the same size as the 64 blocks it replaces - 1152 bytes - so the weight bytes
          moved per token are unchanged. Only their order differs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both bit orders live behind one function that the repack writer and every kernel reader
          call, so the layout cannot drift between them. A numeric test alone would never catch a
          mistake there: writer and reader would share the bug and it would cancel.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============== MODELS ============== -->
<section class="sec" id="models">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">05</span><h2>It does not depend on model size</h2></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The obvious objection to any kernel result is that it was tuned for one shape. Normalizing
        each measurement by the number of weights in its node answers that directly: if the win
        came from a lucky shape, the three bars in each row would differ.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="figbox">
      {{FIG:models}}
      <p class="figcap">
        <b>Same four kernels, three model sizes, one axis.</b> Within each stage the three models
        land on the same cost per weight - across a 12x range of node size and two different
        architectures. The optimization is a property of the layout, not of a tuned shape.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="pull">
      <p>
        The upstream kernel costs about 1.72 cycles per weight regardless of model. The optimized
        one costs about 0.15. That ratio is the whole result, and it holds everywhere we measured.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============== METHOD ============== -->
<section class="sec" id="method">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">06</span><h2>How the numbers were taken</h2></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The core does not exist in silicon yet, so every cycle count here comes from RTL. That
        raises an obvious problem: RTL simulation runs at kilohertz, and the kernel worth
        measuring sits nine minutes of model loading deep inside a 497-node graph.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>andesim</strong> resolves it by splitting the run. A functional model executes the
        program at full speed; when it reaches a marker placed around one MUL_MAT node, it hands
        the machine state to the cycle-accurate engine, which measures that node and nothing else.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="figbox">
      {{FIG:handoff}}
      <p class="figcap">
        <b>One hybrid run, drawn to its real time split.</b> The functional leg loads 3.8 GB and
        executes 58 matmuls in about six minutes; the RTL leg spends three times that on the
        single node under the marker. Measuring the whole token at this fidelity would take
        twelve days.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <p>
        The numbers are reproducible to the cycle: re-running the ladder after a full engine
        rebuild returned six of seven figures bit-identical. That precision is what makes small
        effects legible - the fourth change above is worth 1.26x, which no wall-clock method on a
        loaded workstation could resolve.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============== MACHINE ============== -->
<section class="sec" id="machine">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">07</span><h2>Appendix: the machine</h2></div>
    <ul class="kv">
      <li><span class="k">Core</span><span class="v">Andes AX46MPV, one hart. Every measurement single-threaded.</span></li>
      <li><span class="k">Vector unit</span><span class="v">VLEN 1024, DLEN 1024, ELEN 64. One register is 128 int8 lanes; at LMUL=2, 64 int32 lanes.</span></li>
      <li><span class="k">Memory</span><span class="v">32 KB L1 data, 4-way, 64-byte lines. No L2. 2 MB L3.</span></li>
      <li><span class="k">Vendor extension</span><span class="v"><code>XAndesVDot</code>, providing <code>nds.vd4dots.vv</code>. Part of the core's stock ISA string, not a custom addition.</span></li>
      <li><span class="k">Measured node</span><span class="v">One MUL_MAT per model, selected by index and confirmed by name and shape in the run log.</span></li>
      <li><span class="k">Correctness</span><span class="v">Every stage is bit-identical to the repacked reference across 13 shape cases, at both layout orders.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

